---
title: "Auth"
description: "Google, GitHub, or Vercel sign in with MongoDB cookie sessions, built on fetch and Node crypto with one class per provider."
---

# Auth

The auth utility signs users in with Google, GitHub, or Vercel and keeps them signed in with a session cookie stored in MongoDB. It talks to the provider with `fetch` and creates tokens with Node's `crypto`, so there is no auth library underneath. Each provider is one class in `src/lib/server/auth` that extends `AuthSession`, so a single object such as `googleAuth` builds the sign in URL, checks the callback, and manages the session.

## Installation

Install the item for your provider. Each one pulls in `auth-base` with the session code and the sign out route, and the MongoDB utility that stores users and sessions.

- Install `auth-google` for Google sign in
- Install `auth-github` for GitHub sign in
- Install `auth-vercel` for Vercel sign in

```pm
npx remote0 add https://plainsv.com/r/auth-google.json
```

Then set your site URL, your MongoDB connection string, and the provider's keys in `.env`.

```bash title=".env"
DOMAIN_URL="http://localhost:5173"
MONGODB_URI=""
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=""
```

```bash title=".env"
DOMAIN_URL="http://localhost:5173"
MONGODB_URI=""
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=""
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=""
```

```bash title=".env"
DOMAIN_URL="http://localhost:5173"
MONGODB_URI=""
VERCEL_CLIENT_ID=""
VERCEL_CLIENT_SECRET=""
```

## Provider setup

Create an OAuth app with your provider, and register its callback route on your `DOMAIN_URL` as the callback URL. Add a second app or URL for production, since providers only redirect to registered URLs.

- Create a Google OAuth client in the Google Cloud console with the redirect URI `http://localhost:5173/api/auth/google/callback`
- Create a GitHub OAuth app in your GitHub developer settings with the callback URL `http://localhost:5173/api/auth/github/callback`
- Create a Vercel app under your team's Settings, Apps, add the authorization callback URL `http://localhost:5173/api/auth/vercel/callback`, enable the `openid`, `email`, and `profile` scopes under Permissions, and set its client authentication to `client_secret_post`
- Copy the client ID and secret into `.env`

## Hook

Auth loads the signed-in user with the provider's `hook`, which you run before your main hook in `src/hooks.server.ts`. On every request it reads the `session` cookie and loads the session and user into `event.locals.sessionData` and `event.locals.userData`. Every provider shares the same `hook`, so use any installed one.

The `init` item installs `src/hooks.server.ts` with one `handle` that adds the `dark` class to `<body>`. Rename it to `mainHandle`, then export a new `handle` that runs `hook` first with `sequence`.

```ts title="src/hooks.server.ts"
import { sequence } from "@sveltejs/kit/hooks";
import { googleAuth } from "#lib/server/auth/google.js";
import type { Handle } from "@sveltejs/kit/hooks";

/** Adds the `dark` class to `<body>` on the server */
const mainHandle: Handle = async ({ event, resolve }) => {
	// Handle `dark` class on body on the server side to avoid flicker from light to dark
	if (event.request.method === "GET" && event.route.id !== null) {
		event.locals.isDarkMode = event.cookies.get("theme") === "dark";
		const response = await resolve(event, { transformPageChunk: ({ html }) => (event.locals.isDarkMode ? html.replace("<body ", '<body class="dark" ') : html) });
		return response;
	}
	return await resolve(event);
};

// Load the signed-in user before the main hook
export const handle = sequence(googleAuth.hook, mainHandle);
```

## Sign in

Link to `/api/auth/google`, `/api/auth/github`, or `/api/auth/vercel` to start sign in. Add `callbackURL` to send the user back to a page after the callback, which defaults to `/`.

```svelte title="+page.svelte"
<a href="/api/auth/google?callbackURL=/account">Sign in with Google</a>
```

The sign in route saves a random `state`, and a PKCE verifier for Google and Vercel, in 10-minute cookies and redirects to the provider. The callback route checks them, exchanges the code for tokens with `validateAuthorizationCode`, and loads the user's verified email and avatar with `getUser`. It then finds or creates the user in MongoDB by email and starts a session. `getUser` throws when the email is not verified, so those users are turned away.

## Read the user

Read `locals.userData` in any server load function or endpoint. It holds the MongoDB user with its `_id`, `email`, and `image`, or `null` when no one is signed in.

```ts title="src/routes/account/+page.server.ts"
import { redirect } from "@sveltejs/kit";

export const load = async ({ locals }) => {
	// If no one is signed in: send them to sign in first.
	if (!locals.userData) redirect(302, "/api/auth/google?callbackURL=/account");
	return { email: locals.userData.email };
};
```

## Sign out

Link to `/api/auth/signout` to delete the session and its cookie and go back to `/`. Add `callbackURL` to go back to a page on your site instead. The route reads the `session` cookie itself, so sign out works even without the hook.

```svelte title="+page.svelte"
<a href="/api/auth/signout?callbackURL=/account">Sign out</a>
```

## Sessions

A session lasts 30 days. The cookie holds a random token, and MongoDB stores only its SHA-256 hash as the session `_id`, so a leaked database cannot be used to sign in. When a session has less than 15 days left, the hook moves its expiry 30 days from now and updates the cookie, so active users stay signed in. Expired sessions, and sessions whose user was deleted, are removed on the next request. Auth cookies are `httpOnly`, and outside the dev server they are only sent over HTTPS.

Change both times, in milliseconds, where the provider creates its instance.

```ts title="src/lib/server/auth/google.ts"
const DAY = 1000 * 60 * 60 * 24;

export const googleAuth = new GoogleAuth({ expiresIn: DAY * 7, refreshWithin: DAY * 2 });
```

## Add a provider

Install another provider item to offer more than one sign in button. All providers share the `session` cookie and the `user` collection, and users keep their account across providers that return the same email, since the callback routes match users by email.

```pm
npx remote0 add https://plainsv.com/r/auth-github.json
```

## Update

Run the install command again to update auth. The CLI asks before it overwrites each file that already exists, and overwriting replaces your changes with the registry version. If you changed the callback endpoint, such as `src/routes/api/auth/google/callback/+server.ts`, skip that file when the CLI asks to overwrite it, or your changes are lost.

```pm
npx remote0 add https://plainsv.com/r/auth-google.json
```

## Files

The auth code lives in `src/lib/server/auth`, and the routes live in `src/routes/api/auth`.

- Find the `AuthSession` base class with sessions, cookies, and the hook in `session.ts`
- Find each provider class in `google.ts`, `github.ts`, and `vercel.ts`
- Find the sign in and `callback` endpoints for each provider in `src/routes/api/auth/google`, `src/routes/api/auth/github`, and `src/routes/api/auth/vercel`
- Find the shared `signout` endpoint in `src/routes/api/auth/signout`

## API

```ts title="src/lib/server/auth/session.ts"
/** Shared session methods that every provider class extends */
class AuthSession {
	constructor(options?: { expiresIn?: number; refreshWithin?: number });
	/** Load the session and user from the `session` cookie into `locals` */
	hook: Handle;
	/** Create session token */
	generateSessionToken(): string;
	/** Create session */
	createSession(token: string, userID: string): Promise<DbCollectionsSchema["session"]>;
	/** Set a cookie that page scripts cannot read and that expires at `expiresAt` */
	setCookie(event: RequestEvent, name: string, value: string, expiresAt: Date): void;
	/** Set session to cookies */
	setSessionTokenCookie(event: RequestEvent, token: string, expiresAt: Date): void;
	/** Delete session token from cookies */
	deleteSessionTokenCookie(event: RequestEvent): void;
	/** Validate user session, refreshing it when it expires within `refreshWithin` */
	validateSessionToken(event: RequestEvent, token: string): Promise<SessionValidationResult>;
	/** Delete session from database */
	invalidateSession(sessionId: string): Promise<void>;
}

/** Session methods without a provider, for code every provider shares, such as the sign out route */
const auth: AuthSession;

/** Verified profile every provider's `getUser` returns */
interface AuthUserProfile {
	id: string;
	email: string;
	image: string;
}
```

```ts title="src/lib/server/auth/google.ts"
const googleAuth: AuthSession & {
	createAuthorizationURL(state: string, codeVerifier: string, scopes: string[], redirectURI: string): URL;
	validateAuthorizationCode(code: string, codeVerifier: string, redirectURI: string): Promise<{ accessToken: string; accessTokenExpiresAt: Date; scopes: string[]; claims: GoogleClaims }>;
	/** Reads the profile from the ID token claims, without another request */
	getUser(tokens: { claims: GoogleClaims }): Promise<AuthUserProfile>;
};
```

```ts title="src/lib/server/auth/github.ts"
const githubAuth: AuthSession & {
	createAuthorizationURL(state: string, scopes: string[], redirectURI?: string): URL;
	validateAuthorizationCode(code: string): Promise<{ accessToken: string; scopes: string[] }>;
	/** Loads the profile and primary verified email from the GitHub API */
	getUser(tokens: { accessToken: string }): Promise<AuthUserProfile>;
};
```

```ts title="src/lib/server/auth/vercel.ts"
const vercelAuth: AuthSession & {
	createAuthorizationURL(state: string, codeVerifier: string, scopes: string[], redirectURI: string): URL;
	validateAuthorizationCode(code: string, codeVerifier: string, redirectURI: string): Promise<{ accessToken: string; accessTokenExpiresAt: Date; scopes: string[] }>;
	/** Loads the profile from the UserInfo endpoint */
	getUser(tokens: { accessToken: string }): Promise<AuthUserProfile>;
};
```
